WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.040 --> 00:00:05.960
This is the Saint Sophia&nbsp;
Russian Orthodox Cathedral.

00:00:05.960 --> 00:00:11.160
Less than a mile across town&nbsp;
— is (Joseph) “Stalin Park.”

00:00:11.160 --> 00:00:20.800
The Winters are freezing cold. The economy,&nbsp;
dominated by oil. Strangely, this is not Russia.

00:00:20.800 --> 00:00:24.920
This is China’s northwestern&nbsp;
province of Heilongjiang,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:24.920 --> 00:00:29.040
located just 50 miles above North Korea.

00:00:29.040 --> 00:00:35.360
Not so long ago, this was the beating heart&nbsp;
of the Chinese economy. It supplied the hard,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:35.360 --> 00:00:42.160
physical resources — coal, oil, and gas&nbsp;
— the country needed to industrialize.

00:00:42.160 --> 00:00:49.040
And thanks to its importance, the region basked&nbsp;
in the warm, golden glow of the Party’s favor.

00:00:49.040 --> 00:00:53.600
This statue commemorates one of Mao’s&nbsp;
“model Socialist workers” nicknamed&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:53.600 --> 00:00:58.720
“Iron Man” for his tireless&nbsp;
work on a nearby oilfield.

00:00:58.720 --> 00:01:02.800
Then, one day, China moved on.

00:01:02.800 --> 00:01:07.440
Sometime around 10 years ago,&nbsp;
the country changed focus.

00:01:07.440 --> 00:01:13.560
Dirty, dangerous, and environmentally&nbsp;
destructive industries were out. Meanwhile,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:13.560 --> 00:01:18.640
technology, innovation, and&nbsp;
high-skill labor were in.

00:01:18.640 --> 00:01:25.200
This was no polite suggestion. Nor was&nbsp;
this transition slow and methodical.

00:01:25.200 --> 00:01:28.520
This sweeping economic restructuring was executed&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:28.520 --> 00:01:33.920
with all the force and velocity of&nbsp;
an unchecked, authoritarian regime.

00:01:33.920 --> 00:01:42.560
The message was unambiguous and unforgiving:&nbsp;
Robots are the future. Adapt or be left behind.

00:01:42.560 --> 00:01:46.520
Foxconn — famous for assembling&nbsp;
the iPhone — began building what&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:46.520 --> 00:01:50.760
it ominously called a “one-million robot army.”

00:01:50.760 --> 00:01:54.800
Taxes were cut by 40% for tech companies.

00:01:54.800 --> 00:02:01.880
And one province announced it would spend 70&nbsp;
billion dollars to replace workers with robots.

00:02:01.880 --> 00:02:04.400
These were not empty threats…

00:02:04.400 --> 00:02:12.080
In 2022, nearly 6 times more industrial robots&nbsp;
were installed in China than any other country.

00:02:12.080 --> 00:02:17.320
And, by nearly every metric, this&nbsp;
transformation has been successful.

00:02:17.320 --> 00:02:24.680
Between 2010 and 2020, its economy doubled&nbsp;
in size and its population grew by 76 million&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:24.680 --> 00:02:31.800
people. China is bigger, stronger, and&nbsp;
richer today than it was a decade ago.

00:02:31.800 --> 00:02:34.520
…But what’s often overlooked amidst this “Rise&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:34.520 --> 00:02:40.200
of China” national renaissance&nbsp;
narrative is who was overlooked.

00:02:40.200 --> 00:02:45.840
Not everyone was invited aboard this&nbsp;
high-speed train to modernity. By&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:45.840 --> 00:02:52.520
very explicitly selecting new ‘winners’,&nbsp;
the Party dismissed others as ‘losers’.

00:02:52.520 --> 00:02:58.880
Just as globalization favored coastal cities&nbsp;
like New York, Los Angeles, and San Francisco,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:58.880 --> 00:03:06.320
China’s new economic order favored rich urban&nbsp;
areas like Shanghai, Hangzhou, and Shenzhen.

00:03:06.320 --> 00:03:09.840
Today’s “model Socialist&nbsp;
workers” are Tesla-driving,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:09.840 --> 00:03:13.920
high-rise-living data scientists and engineers.

00:03:13.920 --> 00:03:18.120
Left behind are China’s West Virginias, Detroits,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:18.120 --> 00:03:23.520
and Pittsburghs — collectively&nbsp;
home to 100 million people.

00:03:23.520 --> 00:03:28.960
These depressed cities remain frozen&nbsp;
in time — relics of an earlier era,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:28.960 --> 00:03:33.840
with their depleted oil fields&nbsp;
and Soviet-era Cathedrals.

00:03:33.840 --> 00:03:37.240
In that same decade between 2010 and 20,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:37.240 --> 00:03:43.960
while most of the country was bursting at the&nbsp;
seams with growth, Heilongjiang actually shrank.

00:03:43.960 --> 00:03:47.240
In 2021, just three and a half children were born&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:47.240 --> 00:03:52.880
there for every 1,000 residents —&nbsp;
less than half the national average.

00:03:52.880 --> 00:03:59.040
And if America’s Rust Belt was&nbsp;
forgotten, China’s was flat-out rejected.

00:03:59.040 --> 00:04:04.680
While Americans are — at least legally — free&nbsp;
to pick up and move to new opportunities,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:04.680 --> 00:04:08.120
Chinese workers have no such luxury.

00:04:08.120 --> 00:04:12.240
Because public services in China are&nbsp;
restricted to your place of birth,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:12.240 --> 00:04:15.200
these migrants can’t access healthcare, pensions,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:15.200 --> 00:04:22.520
or education in their new cities. Even their&nbsp;
children are banned from attending local schools.

00:04:22.520 --> 00:04:26.480
In other words, those unlucky enough&nbsp;
to be born in China’s Rust Belt&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:26.480 --> 00:04:31.560
today are permanent second-class&nbsp;
citizens in their own country.

00:04:31.560 --> 00:04:36.160
So, here’s a puzzle: Why aren’t they angry?

00:04:36.160 --> 00:04:38.440
Why, after being replaced by robots,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:38.440 --> 00:04:43.400
do these same workers maintain such&nbsp;
positive views about automation?

00:04:43.400 --> 00:04:47.680
Out of 54 countries, China ranks&nbsp;
second-highest in agreement that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:47.680 --> 00:04:52.240
“more emphasis on technology is&nbsp;
a good thing” and fourth-highest&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:52.240 --> 00:04:57.520
for “science and technology bring more&nbsp;
opportunities for the next generation.”

00:04:57.520 --> 00:05:01.360
China is twice as optimistic&nbsp;
about the future as any other&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:01.360 --> 00:05:06.240
country and six times as optimistic as the U.S.

00:05:06.240 --> 00:05:07.760
Why?

00:05:07.760 --> 00:05:16.280
Sponsored by Nebula, the exclusive home to&nbsp;
“China, Actually”, my seven-part Original series.

00:05:16.280 --> 00:05:19.960
A lot has changed in China&nbsp;
over the last hundred years,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:19.960 --> 00:05:27.000
but one thing has not: its objective to&nbsp;
“catch up and surpass” the United States.

00:05:27.000 --> 00:05:32.680
It’s hard to say where this competitive&nbsp;
mindset originates. But we do know that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:32.680 --> 00:05:37.720
Chinese leaders are big believers&nbsp;
in grand historical narratives.

00:05:37.720 --> 00:05:44.600
Xi Jinping loves to repeat clichés like “the&nbsp;
East is rising and the West is declining.”

00:05:44.600 --> 00:05:49.680
In the Party’s version of history, ‘Western’&nbsp;
imperialist powers once exploited their advanced&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:49.680 --> 00:05:55.960
technology — firearms and naval instruments,&nbsp;
for example — to humiliate the Chinese empire.

00:05:55.960 --> 00:06:03.000
The British stole Hong Kong, the Portuguese,&nbsp;
Macau, and the French, among others, Shanghai.

00:06:03.000 --> 00:06:08.480
These traumatic chapters, it seems, led&nbsp;
Chinese leaders to view history as a one-way,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:08.480 --> 00:06:14.360
zero-sum, linear ‘race’. A&nbsp;
race it’s currently losing.

00:06:14.360 --> 00:06:16.600
To overtake the U.S., therefore,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:16.600 --> 00:06:22.440
it needs to somehow accelerate — to&nbsp;
employ some kind of “force multiplier.”

00:06:22.440 --> 00:06:27.560
China calls this “overtaking on a&nbsp;
bend”. While a more cautious driver&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:27.560 --> 00:06:32.200
like Washington might slow down as it&nbsp;
approaches a sharp curve in the road,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:32.200 --> 00:06:36.360
Beijing, on the other hand,&nbsp;
must fearlessly accelerate.

00:06:36.360 --> 00:06:42.520
Until recently, this meant overlooking the&nbsp;
consequences of rapid industrialization. If&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:42.520 --> 00:06:49.160
America enforced labor laws, China saw its&nbsp;
asymmetrical advantage as ignoring them.

00:06:49.160 --> 00:06:53.880
A culture of “build fast and&nbsp;
break things” at a national scale.

00:06:53.880 --> 00:06:56.800
It also meant quickly and&nbsp;
indiscriminately adopting&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:56.800 --> 00:07:02.880
Western technology — especially&nbsp;
after Mao’s death in the 1970s.

00:07:02.880 --> 00:07:06.600
The Party, at the time, faced a serious problem:

00:07:06.600 --> 00:07:11.480
On one hand, everyone could see that Mao’s&nbsp;
Great Leap Forward had been an unmitigated&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:11.480 --> 00:07:16.280
disaster — accomplishing little and&nbsp;
killing millions in the process.

00:07:16.280 --> 00:07:18.560
On the other, it certainly couldn’t condemn its&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:18.560 --> 00:07:23.160
own founder and ‘hero’ — the&nbsp;
literal face of the Party.

00:07:23.160 --> 00:07:28.800
Ultimately, the way it lawyered itself out&nbsp;
of this embarrassing dilemma went like this:&nbsp;

00:07:28.800 --> 00:07:34.160
While Mao’s goals were noble,&nbsp;
his methods may have been flawed.

00:07:34.160 --> 00:07:38.880
With a few tweaks here and there,&nbsp;
China would be back on track.

00:07:38.880 --> 00:07:43.360
The Communist Party was still relevant&nbsp;
because only it had the scientific and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:43.360 --> 00:07:47.280
technocratic expertise to make those adjustments.

00:07:47.280 --> 00:07:53.840
Its performance would now be based more on&nbsp;
its material than its ideological success.

00:07:53.840 --> 00:07:57.520
And this ‘success’ would largely be&nbsp;
measured by the number of smartphones&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:57.520 --> 00:08:04.000
in people’s hands, robots on factory&nbsp;
floors, and miles of high-speed rail.

00:08:04.000 --> 00:08:09.320
In the process, technology took&nbsp;
on an almost supernatural aura. It&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:09.320 --> 00:08:16.640
became the “magic bullet” to all of China’s&nbsp;
problems — its state religion, if you will.

00:08:16.640 --> 00:08:21.840
Naturally, this led to lighting-fast&nbsp;
adoption. The country went straight from&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:21.840 --> 00:08:28.680
cash to mobile payments, from fax machines&nbsp;
to iPhones. In its quest to catch up,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:28.680 --> 00:08:34.200
it skipped right over checks, credit&nbsp;
cards, pagers, and Blackberries.

00:08:34.200 --> 00:08:37.400
But this infatuation with technology also made it&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:37.400 --> 00:08:43.480
vulnerable to digital snake oil —&nbsp;
waste, fraud, and false promises.

00:08:43.480 --> 00:08:47.680
To see why, watch how all&nbsp;
this plays out on the ground,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:47.680 --> 00:08:54.720
as these grand aspirations trickle down through&nbsp;
the Party's 40-million-person bureaucracy…

00:08:54.720 --> 00:09:00.840
First, Xi Jinping wakes up one day&nbsp;
and declares “Robots are the future!”

00:09:00.840 --> 00:09:06.280
But although his authority is unlimited,&nbsp;
his ability to execute this vision is&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:06.280 --> 00:09:11.880
limited — he himself can’t make&nbsp;
or buy or regulate these robots.

00:09:11.880 --> 00:09:18.800
The Central government, therefore, delegates the&nbsp;
‘how’ to its ‘what’ down to local governments.

00:09:18.800 --> 00:09:23.600
To incentivize their cooperation,&nbsp;
Beijing promotes and demotes these&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:23.600 --> 00:09:29.600
officials based on quantifiable metrics&nbsp;
— say, how many robots they install.

00:09:29.600 --> 00:09:35.560
And, eager to get that corner office,&nbsp;
boy, will they install some robots!

00:09:35.560 --> 00:09:41.600
As a rule, when China’s goals are easily measured&nbsp;
— file more patents, graduate more engineers,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:41.600 --> 00:09:47.560
or produce cheaper solar panels — you can&nbsp;
pretty confidently bet on its success.

00:09:47.560 --> 00:09:52.360
Indeed, as you can see, digital&nbsp;
technology has grown from 14% of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:52.360 --> 00:09:59.200
its economy in 2005 to a remarkable 39% in 2020.

00:09:59.200 --> 00:10:04.800
That’s what China is good at. These&nbsp;
incentives allow it to go from napkin&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:04.800 --> 00:10:09.480
sketch to completed skyscraper in no time flat.

00:10:09.480 --> 00:10:14.520
But! When its goals are intangible or&nbsp;
more subjective — like winning hearts&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:14.520 --> 00:10:18.600
and minds around the globe — it tends to struggle.

00:10:18.600 --> 00:10:25.640
It’s easy to build more robots. It’s a lot&nbsp;
harder to make sure they’re put to good use.

00:10:25.640 --> 00:10:32.200
Now, make no mistake: much of this&nbsp;
39% of China’s economy is productive.

00:10:32.200 --> 00:10:36.840
As the government dumped billions of dollars&nbsp;
worth of subsidies on the tech sector,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:36.840 --> 00:10:42.080
some of it landed on your Huawei’s,&nbsp;
your TikTok’s, your Xiaomi’s.

00:10:42.080 --> 00:10:48.160
But for every TikTok, there are 10 million&nbsp;
terminally-unprofitable metaverse startups.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:48.160 --> 00:10:54.080
For every Huawei, there are 20 billion&nbsp;
nebulous “smart city” initiatives.

00:10:54.080 --> 00:10:56.880
You name a gimmick, scam, or buzzword,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:56.880 --> 00:11:03.040
and the Chinese government has almost certainly&nbsp;
invested an alarming number of zeros in it.

00:11:03.040 --> 00:11:05.840
Last July, for instance, the city of Shanghai&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:05.840 --> 00:11:10.440
announced it would spend over one&nbsp;
billion dollars on the metaverse.

00:11:10.440 --> 00:11:15.600
A few years earlier, this “futuristic&nbsp;
bus” went viral for “solving” the problem&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:15.600 --> 00:11:21.160
of gridlock by gliding over traffic.&nbsp;
Unfortunately, it was later exposed as&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:21.160 --> 00:11:28.200
a pyramid scheme — but not before 5 Chinese&nbsp;
cities signed contracts with the company.

00:11:28.200 --> 00:11:34.480
Now, no country is immune to misplaced&nbsp;
optimism. Remember Solar Roadways?

00:11:34.480 --> 00:11:40.600
In fact, who pioneered the metaverse&nbsp;
craze but Facebook — an American company!

00:11:40.600 --> 00:11:46.040
But if Silicon Valley wins the award for&nbsp;
inventing the most cutting-edge snake oil,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:46.040 --> 00:11:49.640
China wins the award for buying the most.

00:11:49.640 --> 00:11:54.720
Time after time after time, China&nbsp;
eagerly imports these wasteful,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:54.720 --> 00:12:00.520
speculative “innovations” and then proceeds&nbsp;
to dig the deepest possible money pit.

00:12:00.520 --> 00:12:06.360
Part of the problem is that private companies take&nbsp;
advantage of the government’s faith in technology.

00:12:06.360 --> 00:12:08.480
Why would they question the usefulness of all&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:08.480 --> 00:12:13.600
these gadgets when they’re being handed&nbsp;
billions of dollars worth of subsidies?&nbsp;

00:12:13.600 --> 00:12:19.320
Assessing the utility of cutting-edge&nbsp;
inventions is hard enough. Doubly so for&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:19.320 --> 00:12:24.760
a 70-year-old with no technical background.&nbsp;
And triply so when you’ve constructed an&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:24.760 --> 00:12:30.960
intricate bureaucracy of 40 million yes-men&nbsp;
with no incentive to give negative feedback.

00:12:30.960 --> 00:12:37.440
As a result, it takes a long time for reality&nbsp;
to catch up with the country’s leaders.

00:12:37.440 --> 00:12:44.520
And, therefore, when it finally does, it often&nbsp;
provokes a swift and brutal overcorrection.

00:12:44.520 --> 00:12:51.320
Recall that it began shifting focus from&nbsp;
heavy industry to tech sometime around 2010.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:51.320 --> 00:12:56.840
For the next decade, China’s Silicon Valley&nbsp;
enjoyed its moment in the spotlight — it,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:56.840 --> 00:13:01.760
like coal, oil, and gas before&nbsp;
it, basked in the Party’s favor.

00:13:01.760 --> 00:13:06.520
Companies like Baidu, Tencent, and&nbsp;
Alibaba exploited this boundless&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:06.520 --> 00:13:11.120
enthusiasm and cheap capital&nbsp;
to grow bigger and bigger.

00:13:11.120 --> 00:13:14.240
They started making riskier bets. They entered&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:14.240 --> 00:13:20.400
wildly unrelated markets. And their&nbsp;
CEOs became dangerously popular.

00:13:20.400 --> 00:13:24.120
Then, after 10 years, the fun&nbsp;
came to a screeching halt as&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:24.120 --> 00:13:27.840
the Party realized the monster it had created.

00:13:27.840 --> 00:13:31.040
And in 2020, it sprung into action,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:31.040 --> 00:13:36.280
breaking up the largest companies and&nbsp;
regulating them like never before.

00:13:36.280 --> 00:13:41.000
To be clear: the Party hasn’t&nbsp;
lost its faith in technology.

00:13:41.000 --> 00:13:44.800
This crackdown was about steering&nbsp;
the industry toward what it calls&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:44.800 --> 00:13:51.920
“high-quality” or “productive” growth. Think less&nbsp;
internet gambling and Fortnite and loan sharks,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:51.920 --> 00:13:56.360
and more semiconductors, EVs, and robots.

00:13:56.360 --> 00:14:01.880
In fact, China really has no choice&nbsp;
but to double down on technology.

00:14:01.880 --> 00:14:08.160
In 2021, its population began to shrink.&nbsp;
And this decline is especially pronounced&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:08.160 --> 00:14:13.400
among the working-age — those roughly 18 to 65.

00:14:13.400 --> 00:14:18.600
Meanwhile, Chinese labor — once among the&nbsp;
cheapest in the world — is now four times&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:18.600 --> 00:14:23.720
as expensive as neighboring countries&nbsp;
like Thailand and the Philippines.

00:14:23.720 --> 00:14:28.680
Unsurprisingly, many companies have responded by&nbsp;
shifting their production to those cheaper places.

00:14:30.120 --> 00:14:35.440
Each year, fewer and fewer Chinese&nbsp;
workers are employed in manufacturing.

00:14:35.440 --> 00:14:40.040
All these factory workers have&nbsp;
to find new jobs somewhere.

00:14:40.040 --> 00:14:44.840
And tech is one of the few&nbsp;
areas of growth. As you can see,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:44.840 --> 00:14:51.840
the decline in manufacturing has coincided with&nbsp;
a rapid rise in labor-intensive service jobs.

00:14:51.840 --> 00:14:57.920
These are your Uber drivers, your live-streamers,&nbsp;
and your DoorDash delivery people.

00:14:57.920 --> 00:15:03.080
So, that’s why the government must&nbsp;
continue embracing technology. But&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:03.080 --> 00:15:08.840
this still leaves one important&nbsp;
question: Why do workers?

00:15:08.840 --> 00:15:11.680
After all, the jump from assembling iPhones to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:11.680 --> 00:15:15.720
delivering meals isn’t all&nbsp;
that great of a promotion.

00:15:15.720 --> 00:15:20.360
These are still low-paid and&nbsp;
surprisingly dangerous jobs.

00:15:20.360 --> 00:15:25.000
Because delayed orders are heavily penalized,&nbsp;
for example, Chinese delivery people are&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:25.000 --> 00:15:32.240
prone to extremely reckless driving. Serious&nbsp;
injuries and even deaths are shockingly common.

00:15:32.240 --> 00:15:37.600
Never mind that these aren’t, strictly&nbsp;
speaking, “jobs”. They’re independent&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:37.600 --> 00:15:42.960
contractor arrangements that can and&nbsp;
do disappear at a moment’s notice.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:42.960 --> 00:15:49.200
China’s largest platforms can change their&nbsp;
contracts with drivers without their consent.

00:15:49.200 --> 00:15:54.240
Now, that’s the most vulnerable end of&nbsp;
the spectrum. But even the luckiest,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:54.240 --> 00:16:00.880
most privileged workers — programmers and&nbsp;
engineers — are overworked and underpaid.

00:16:00.880 --> 00:16:09.640
Some work what’s widely known in China as a “996&nbsp;
schedule”, meaning 9am to 9pm, six days a week.

00:16:09.640 --> 00:16:13.120
In other words, workers are&nbsp;
either excluded from the labor&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:13.120 --> 00:16:18.400
market — like we saw in Heilongjiang&nbsp;
— or abused, as in the case of urban,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:18.400 --> 00:16:25.680
highly-educated programmers. Neither are paid&nbsp;
as well as their counterparts across the globe.

00:16:25.680 --> 00:16:32.040
When even the “winners” are treated so poorly,&nbsp;
one wonders why workers don’t complain.

00:16:32.040 --> 00:16:37.920
The obvious answer, of course, is that&nbsp;
they do, yet their voices are censored.

00:16:37.920 --> 00:16:42.840
Another explanation is that the public&nbsp;
only hears “good stories” about technology,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:42.840 --> 00:16:46.320
thanks to the Party’s control of the media.

00:16:46.320 --> 00:16:53.720
Both these things are true. There’s no doubt&nbsp;
censorship and propaganda play a massive role.

00:16:53.720 --> 00:16:59.960
But theories alone are no substitute&nbsp;
for real-world, on-the-ground research.

00:16:59.960 --> 00:17:04.960
At some point, it helps to just&nbsp;
ask. And that’s exactly what&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:04.960 --> 00:17:08.760
Ya-Wen Lei did for her book “The Gilded Cage”.

00:17:08.760 --> 00:17:11.400
What she found, in interview after interview,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:11.400 --> 00:17:17.520
is that Chinese programmers are aware of their&nbsp;
unusually stressful working conditions. But,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:17.520 --> 00:17:24.480
they situate their personal hardships within&nbsp;
a larger context: the nation’s development.

00:17:24.480 --> 00:17:27.920
One engineer said, quote, “If&nbsp;
Chinese people worked from 9&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:27.920 --> 00:17:33.200
to 5 like people in developed countries,&nbsp;
we wouldn’t have so many accomplishments”

00:17:33.200 --> 00:17:37.880
And another, “China remains a developing&nbsp;
country… I don't think… the government can&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:37.880 --> 00:17:42.280
do anything if we want to catch&nbsp;
up with developed countries.”

00:17:42.280 --> 00:17:50.520
Put differently, their low monetary wages are&nbsp;
offset by a kind of psychological wage — pride.

00:17:50.520 --> 00:17:53.880
This is why Xi Jinping loves to repeat platitudes&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:53.880 --> 00:17:58.600
like “the East is rising and the West&nbsp;
is declining” — these narratives buy&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:58.600 --> 00:18:04.960
the Party incredible leeway. All is&nbsp;
forgiven in the name of “progress”.

00:18:04.960 --> 00:18:11.560
Chinese tech workers aren’t exploited, they’re&nbsp;
part of something bigger than themselves!

00:18:11.560 --> 00:18:16.000
Many of the darkest chapters of Chinese&nbsp;
history have been justified by this vague&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:16.000 --> 00:18:21.840
notion of “national progress”, from the&nbsp;
Great Leap Forward to the One-Child Policy.

00:18:21.840 --> 00:18:26.280
Even today, it’s widely assumed that&nbsp;
the latter was necessary, effective, and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:26.280 --> 00:18:31.240
helpful — three myths that I challenge in&nbsp;
the latest episode of my series “China,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:31.240 --> 00:18:38.120
Actually”. In it, we also explore questions like&nbsp;
“How was the policy enforced?”, “Who was and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:38.120 --> 00:18:44.480
wasn’t affected?”, and how does China’s birth&nbsp;
rate compare to nearby countries like Taiwan?

00:18:44.480 --> 00:18:48.360
The One Child Policy was among&nbsp;
humanity’s most ambitious attempts&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:48.360 --> 00:18:53.200
at social engineering and yet so&nbsp;
little is known about it today.

